Project status — 10 November 2026

The project is a simulation-first peg-in-hole benchmark for studying
force-aware residual control around a fixed pi0.5 policy. Stages A and B have
working prototypes but are not fully validated; Stage C is in progress.
Residual-policy training and real-robot evaluation have not been completed.

Working
- MuJoCo Panda simulation with joint control, torque feedback and observers,
  wrist force sensing, and classical force/compliance controllers.
- Peg, gripper, table, hole and cameras; insertion, seating, force, drop,
  workspace and timeout checks. Trials start with the peg already grasped.
- pi0.5-DROID observation/action adapters and guarded simulation rollouts.
- Classical demonstration and policy-failure recording, including
  physics-step controls and replay states linked to camera samples.

Verified
- On 10 held-out simulation seeds, position, hybrid and impedance baselines
  succeeded in 8, 10 and 9 trials respectively. These small samples do not
  establish comparative performance.
- The real pi0.5 checkpoint ran locally in simulation; one recorded contact
  trial ended in a dropped peg, not a successful insertion.
- A five-trial classical pilot recorded three training successes, one
  validation timeout and one test success. Replaying recorded controls
  reproduced the sampled endpoints in the tested simulator.
- Motion-derived imitation actions were not accurate enough: training-trial
  95th-percentile peg-tip errors were 0.125–0.153 mm, above the nominal
  0.08 mm radial clearance. No imitation baseline has been trained.


    The classical controller and π0.5 use different kinds of actions:

The classical controller applies actuator controls to move the arm.
π0.5 outputs normalized joint-velocity commands.

- The most recent full test run passed 101 tests with one skipped; the
  optional OpenPI client environment passed 27 focused tests.

Still needed
- Finalize task protocol, simulation geometry/contact and benchmark
  validation; gather a larger, representative dataset.
- Validate a suitable classical-to-policy action mapping with continuous,
  held-out rollouts; define complete training-only normalization and train
  the initial imitation baseline.
- Verify checkpoint weight terms, then implement and evaluate the residual
  policy and its force-observation/reward ablations.
- Before any hardware use, independently validate sensing, timing, controls
  and safety. The simulation's 5 N force stop is checked after a physics
  step, so it is not a force cap; real-robot testing remains deferred.

See mujoco_force_control/README.md for commands, data contracts and limits,
and thoughts for the research plan.

Running on the CU Boulder Alpine cluster

Alpine can run simulation trials now and GPU training once the training code
and validated action labels exist. An active CURC account and Alpine
allocation are required. Log in and select the Alpine scheduler:

  ssh YOUR_USERNAME@login.rc.colorado.edu
  module load slurm/alpine

After committing and pushing the project, clone it on Alpine:

  git clone https://github.com/amhe1346/Special-topics.git
  cd Special-topics/mujoco_force_control

Authenticate if the repository is private. Create Python environments on
Alpine rather than copying local environments; use `module avail python` to
find an available Python module. Following CURC's installation guidance, use
an appropriate compute-node allocation to install project dependencies:

  python3 -m venv .venv
  .venv/bin/python -m pip install -r requirements.txt

Do not run substantial computations on login nodes. For an initial CPU
benchmark, create `sim.sbatch` in mujoco_force_control, replace
YOUR_ALLOCATION with your actual allocation, and submit it from that directory:

  #!/bin/bash
  #SBATCH --job-name=peg-sim
  #SBATCH --account=YOUR_ALLOCATION
  #SBATCH --partition=acpu
  #SBATCH --qos=cpu-normal
  #SBATCH --nodes=1
  #SBATCH --ntasks=1
  #SBATCH --cpus-per-task=1
  #SBATCH --mem=4G
  #SBATCH --time=01:00:00
  #SBATCH --output=peg-sim-%j.log

  set -euo pipefail
  cd "$SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR"
  export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1
  export OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1
  .venv/bin/python benchmark_insertion.py \
    --method all --trials 3 --seed 0 > "simulation-${SLURM_JOB_ID}.jsonl"

  module load slurm/alpine
  sbatch sim.sbatch
  squeue -u "$USER"

The script writes trial summaries as JSONL and Slurm output to a log file.
The resource request is an unmeasured starting point. For larger batches,
use Slurm job arrays with distinct seeds and output paths.

Camera recording requires a working headless renderer. Test this command
inside an NVIDIA GPU allocation; the output directory must not already exist:

  MUJOCO_GL=egl .venv/bin/python collect_stage_c.py \
    --output "results/alpine-pilot-${SLURM_JOB_ID}"

EGL support depends on the node's graphics and driver environment. The
collector currently uses a fixed five-trial split, so running multiple
identical jobs does not produce new seed ranges.

An example GPU request, subject to allocation access and availability:

  #SBATCH --account=YOUR_ALLOCATION
  #SBATCH --partition=aa100
  #SBATCH --qos=gpu-normal
  #SBATCH --gres=gpu:a100-40gb:1
  #SBATCH --cpus-per-task=4
  #SBATCH --mem=32G
  #SBATCH --time=02:00:00

The local pi0.5 inference run used roughly 20 GB of GPU memory; a 40 GB GPU
is a starting point, not a training guarantee. The official OpenPI server and
client require separate environments; see the project README. Run both on the
same allocated node with the server bound to localhost. Use suitable
project/scratch storage for checkpoints and datasets, observe CURC quotas and
retention policies, and back up important outputs.

Before GPU training, validate the action conversion on held-out continuous
rollouts, implement the imitation/residual training and ablations, define
normalization and checkpointing, and verify checkpoint weight terms.

